1
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:19,542
- Hello, how are you?
- I'm fine. How about you? Good?

2
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:25,737
Here it is. MAN:
Thank you very much.

3
00:01:25,819 --> 00:01:28,846
So Judge Hamner calls me
and Hogan into his chambers.

4
00:01:29,022 --> 00:01:31,423
- What, out of the blue?
- Right after he adjourns court.

5
00:01:31,592 --> 00:01:34,323
Dick, my head was spinning. I
had no idea what this was about.

6
00:01:34,494 --> 00:01:36,258
- What happens?
- Soon as we closed the door,

7
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:38,990
Hamner turns to Hogan,
points his finger at Hogan

8
00:01:39,166 --> 00:01:41,863
and says, "Counselor, it is
your God-given and legal right

9
00:01:42,035 --> 00:01:44,300
to object in my
courtroom. But so help me,

10
00:01:44,471 --> 00:01:46,531
if you utter another
groundless objection,

11
00:01:46,707 --> 00:01:48,027
I am going to
turn to your client

12
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:50,269
and strongly recommend
that he discharge you

13
00:01:50,444 --> 00:01:53,437
and then hold you while
he decks you. Got it?

14
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:56,140
Ha, ha, ha. That's
priceless. Ha, ha, ha.

15
00:01:56,316 --> 00:01:57,340
Hello, Brad.

16
00:01:57,517 --> 00:02:01,613
Sorry. Settlement conference,
ran longer than I thought.

17
00:02:01,788 --> 00:02:03,416
- How did it go?
- Lousy.

18
00:02:03,590 --> 00:02:04,901
What did the homeowners ask for?

19
00:02:04,925 --> 00:02:06,553
Twenty million.

20
00:02:07,628 --> 00:02:09,256
Who are they kidding?

21
00:02:09,429 --> 00:02:13,264
Andros Chemical dumped toxic
waste all over that development, boys.

22
00:02:13,433 --> 00:02:17,029
- The area is contaminated.
- We realize people got sick, Brad.

23
00:02:17,204 --> 00:02:22,006
They got cancer, Dick,
six times the national rate.

24
00:02:22,409 --> 00:02:24,640
Personally, I think
the jury is gonna award

25
00:02:24,811 --> 00:02:27,076
that homeowners group
upwards of 50 million.

26
00:02:29,116 --> 00:02:30,812
I recommended to
Andros that they settle.

27
00:02:30,984 --> 00:02:32,452
I'm not sure that
was wise, Brad.

28
00:02:32,619 --> 00:02:34,679
I'm surprised they
didn't fire us on the spot.

29
00:02:35,789 --> 00:02:38,691
I worked my hide four
months on this case.

30
00:02:38,859 --> 00:02:40,919
If you think you can
do better, be my guest.

31
00:02:41,094 --> 00:02:43,359
- Brad, we're not second-guessing you.
- Like hell.

32
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:46,830
Something else. I want that associate
you saddled me with off the case.

33
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,799
Brad, you're one of the people
who hired Todd McCormick.

34
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:51,063
So I made a mistake.

35
00:02:51,238 --> 00:02:54,572
And I'm not talking about his linen
suit or the stereo system in his office.

36
00:02:54,741 --> 00:02:56,437
- He's unconventional.
- He's worthless.

37
00:02:56,610 --> 00:02:59,409
Brad, I think
you're overreacting.

38
00:02:59,579 --> 00:03:00,603
Now, come on.

39
00:03:00,981 --> 00:03:03,610
Let's give him another chance.

40
00:03:07,654 --> 00:03:09,452
And over the
course of this trial,

41
00:03:09,623 --> 00:03:12,058
you'll be presented
with irrefutable evidence,

42
00:03:12,225 --> 00:03:17,687
indicating that high, lethal levels
of TriOxin, a deadly contaminant,

43
00:03:18,298 --> 00:03:20,927
were and still
continues to be present

44
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:23,536
in the soil in this community.

45
00:03:23,937 --> 00:03:28,432
A chemical recklessly
and cavalierly dumped

46
00:03:29,309 --> 00:03:31,175
by Andros Chemical Corporation.

47
00:03:31,344 --> 00:03:33,472
These people, they
have no conscience.

48
00:03:33,647 --> 00:03:36,913
There's only one way to get
the attention of toxic polluters

49
00:03:37,084 --> 00:03:40,384
like Andros Chemical,
and that's to punish them.

50
00:03:41,188 --> 00:03:46,786
Punish them with the highest possible
monetary judgment against them.

51
00:03:47,627 --> 00:03:49,152
Thank you.

52
00:03:51,631 --> 00:03:55,432
And while counsel for the
plaintiff will try to paint the picture

53
00:03:55,602 --> 00:03:59,972
of Andros Chemical as a
group of evil, negligent polluters,

54
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:03,076
evidence will prove
quite to the contrary.

55
00:04:03,510 --> 00:04:07,675
The truth is at the time the
disposal of TriOxin occurred,

56
00:04:07,848 --> 00:04:10,750
Andros Chemical was operating
well within safety standards

57
00:04:10,917 --> 00:04:14,115
required by law. No
one, not Andros Chemical,

58
00:04:14,554 --> 00:04:18,321
not even the Environmental
Protection Agency knew at the time

59
00:04:18,492 --> 00:04:21,155
that the health and the safety
of the people of this community

60
00:04:21,328 --> 00:04:23,456
was being jeopardized.

61
00:04:24,998 --> 00:04:29,265
Andros Chemical abided by
the law set by your legislators.

62
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:32,767
Is it not then patently
unfair to punish someone

63
00:04:32,939 --> 00:04:35,170
who adheres to the
laws that govern them?

64
00:04:37,844 --> 00:04:38,903
The hour is late.

65
00:04:39,079 --> 00:04:41,878
This court will stand in recess
until 10:00 tomorrow morning.

66
00:04:46,419 --> 00:04:48,581
I'll call you this evening.

67
00:04:48,822 --> 00:04:49,881
Nice going, Brad.

68
00:04:50,056 --> 00:04:53,220
Well, thanks, Todd, but
we're gonna get buried.

69
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:56,420
Listen, I'm still researching other
areas of TriOxin contamination

70
00:04:56,596 --> 00:05:00,089
where the incidents report the
facts in cancer are lower than this.

71
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:03,767
Well, you plan on getting me
this information for this trial?

72
00:05:04,738 --> 00:05:06,138
It's not easy, Brad.

73
00:05:06,306 --> 00:05:08,298
Look, I don't wanna
hear your bellyaching.

74
00:05:08,475 --> 00:05:10,808
Just get me the information.

75
00:05:28,762 --> 00:05:31,823
- Hi.
- The car is down the block.

76
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:33,591
How's your hangover?

77
00:05:36,136 --> 00:05:38,298
Trial is pretty interesting.

78
00:05:41,041 --> 00:05:43,169
Well, obviously,
you're still mad.

79
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:44,675
Yeah, I'm still mad.

80
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:46,279
They're my best friends, Janet.

81
00:05:46,446 --> 00:05:49,678
I'm aware of that, Gary. You spend
four nights a week drinking with them.

82
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:52,547
You know, their wives
don't get on their case

83
00:05:52,719 --> 00:05:54,517
every time they
stay out late with me.

84
00:05:54,888 --> 00:05:57,619
Don't pretend you don't
know what this is about.

85
00:05:57,791 --> 00:06:00,920
How many of your buddies come
home smelling like a French whorehouse?

86
00:06:01,094 --> 00:06:02,494
They also smelled
like cigarettes.

87
00:06:02,662 --> 00:06:04,358
Why don't you
accuse me of smoking?

88
00:06:04,631 --> 00:06:07,465
Because cigarettes were
never one of your weaknesses.

89
00:06:08,768 --> 00:06:10,828
- Janet, come on.
- I don't wanna talk about it.

90
00:06:11,004 --> 00:06:12,905
- I'm sorry.
- Forget it.

91
00:06:13,073 --> 00:06:16,737
I'm an idiot. I think we've
established that by now, haven't we?

92
00:06:23,083 --> 00:06:25,143
I'm not fooling around.

93
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:28,855
You better not be.

94
00:06:48,108 --> 00:06:51,909
Where are those studies?
Turn that damn thing off!

95
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:55,374
Where are those
studies you promised me?

96
00:06:55,548 --> 00:06:58,848
Uh, I'm sorry, Brad, I'm
not having much luck.

97
00:06:59,552 --> 00:07:02,920
Look, this is the telephone number
for the president of Andros Chemical.

98
00:07:03,356 --> 00:07:05,552
I want you to call him up
and I want you to tell him

99
00:07:05,725 --> 00:07:08,559
that the luck upon which we
have based our entire defense

100
00:07:08,728 --> 00:07:10,094
seems to be eluding us.

101
00:07:10,263 --> 00:07:12,823
Look, Brad, I know you think
I've just been sitting here...

102
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:14,199
You have given me no reason...

103
00:07:14,301 --> 00:07:17,135
Wait, wait, wait. Brad, look,
look, look. Here. Feast your eyes.

104
00:07:17,304 --> 00:07:21,139
Twelve cases of TriOxin contamination
over three different continents.

105
00:07:21,308 --> 00:07:23,777
The levels of toxicity are
almost identical to our case.

106
00:07:23,944 --> 00:07:26,846
The incidence of cancer is the
same, infant mortality is the same.

107
00:07:27,013 --> 00:07:29,539
We do not pay you to
prove our opposition's case.

108
00:07:29,716 --> 00:07:32,550
The evidence you're
looking for doesn't exist, Brad.

109
00:07:33,053 --> 00:07:36,990
Andros Chemical broke the
law. Don't blame me for that.

110
00:07:37,424 --> 00:07:39,984
You haven't been around long
enough to get a severance package.

111
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:44,029
Just clean out your desk and don't
show your face around here again.

112
00:07:49,502 --> 00:07:52,438
Ha, ha, sweet. Yes!

113
00:07:55,208 --> 00:07:57,234
Maybe I should
give up my day job.

114
00:07:57,410 --> 00:08:00,209
Wait a minute. Don't you have
to get a day job to give it up?

115
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,860
- Oh, yeah, you're right.
- Double or nothing, what do you say?

116
00:08:03,016 --> 00:08:04,416
- No. No.
- Come on. Come on.

117
00:08:04,584 --> 00:08:05,608
- No way.
- Come on.

118
00:08:05,785 --> 00:08:07,651
It's always double or
nothing with you. Pay up.

119
00:08:07,821 --> 00:08:09,813
- A bet is a bet.
- That's right, pay the piper.

120
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:11,925
Put up or shut up, huh?

121
00:08:13,693 --> 00:08:17,027
You've never shot that well before
in your entire life. Heh, heh, heh.

122
00:08:17,197 --> 00:08:20,037
That's why we couldn't play again.
I didn't have any more of that in me.

123
00:08:21,134 --> 00:08:25,504
Hey, Gary. Stool number five can't
take her eyes off you, man. Take a look.

124
00:08:26,639 --> 00:08:28,972
So why you? You're
not that good-looking.

125
00:08:29,142 --> 00:08:31,270
- Yeah, I am. ALL:
Heh, heh, heh.

126
00:08:31,444 --> 00:08:33,589
Well, gentlemen, the
party was fun while it lasted.

127
00:08:33,613 --> 00:08:36,139
Oh, no, no, no. I'm in enough
trouble with Janet as it is.

128
00:08:36,316 --> 00:08:39,013
Gary, this isn't like you.
- Yeah, well...

129
00:08:39,185 --> 00:08:41,347
Hey, you get her phone number,

130
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:43,001
I'm buying beers
the rest of the week.

131
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:46,322
Excuse me. Did you
say "rest of the week"?

132
00:08:46,493 --> 00:08:48,428
Rest of the week. GARY: Ah.

133
00:08:48,595 --> 00:08:50,120
- Well?
- I'm getting thirsty.

134
00:08:50,296 --> 00:08:52,265
- Oh, yeah.
- I'm thirsty.

135
00:08:57,003 --> 00:08:59,529
- Oh, he's unbelievable.
- He's doing it.

136
00:09:01,541 --> 00:09:03,237
Hi.

137
00:09:07,147 --> 00:09:08,740
How you doing?

138
00:09:09,816 --> 00:09:11,216
Fine.

139
00:09:11,785 --> 00:09:14,016
It's this kind of
scintillating conversation

140
00:09:14,187 --> 00:09:16,452
that's become my trademark. Heh.

141
00:09:16,890 --> 00:09:18,085
You're very good.

142
00:09:19,993 --> 00:09:22,258
Obviously, my
reputation precedes me.

143
00:09:22,429 --> 00:09:23,692
At darts.

144
00:09:25,365 --> 00:09:26,389
Oh.

145
00:09:28,735 --> 00:09:30,727
- Oh, look at that.
- Look at that.

146
00:09:37,143 --> 00:09:39,305
I hope you got a
raise recently, pal.

147
00:09:43,216 --> 00:09:46,709
- Well, I will certainly call you.
- Okay.

148
00:09:47,921 --> 00:09:49,321
Bye.

149
00:09:52,425 --> 00:09:54,394
Gary, come over.

150
00:09:57,430 --> 00:09:59,262
Coming, coming, coming.

151
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:02,395
Hey, Nellie.

152
00:10:09,309 --> 00:10:10,402
Hi, Mr. Matlock.

153
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:13,246
- Well, as I live and breathe.
- It's Todd McCormick.

154
00:10:13,413 --> 00:10:15,279
I applied for a job with
you here years ago.

155
00:10:15,448 --> 00:10:16,973
- I remember.
- You do?

156
00:10:17,150 --> 00:10:20,018
Yeah. You're the
only lawyer that I know,

157
00:10:20,186 --> 00:10:22,382
looking for a job, ever asked me

158
00:10:22,555 --> 00:10:25,320
where my season tickets for
the Hawks and the Braves were.

159
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:29,256
- Mind if...? Can I come in?
- Come on in.

160
00:10:29,429 --> 00:10:32,399
Thank you. Nice to see you.

161
00:10:33,633 --> 00:10:37,195
- What is that? Crepe paper?
- It's linen.

162
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:40,135
- It must wrinkle a ton.
- Yeah.

163
00:10:40,306 --> 00:10:43,003
So where did you wind up?
Want a bottle of pop or something?

164
00:10:43,176 --> 00:10:45,941
No, thanks. Over
at Gavin $ Howard.

165
00:10:46,112 --> 00:10:49,139
- It's a good firm.
- Yeah. Just left them, actually.

166
00:10:49,315 --> 00:10:50,339
Oh, yeah?

167
00:10:50,517 --> 00:10:52,611
I went to law school
because I wanted

168
00:10:52,785 --> 00:10:54,777
to defend the
wrongly-accused, the little guy.

169
00:10:54,954 --> 00:10:56,183
Who do I end up defending?

170
00:10:56,356 --> 00:10:58,825
- Toxic polluters.
- Oh.

171
00:10:58,992 --> 00:11:00,585
You got fired.

172
00:11:00,994 --> 00:11:04,829
It was a mutual thing. Gavin $
Howard is a pretty conservative firm,

173
00:11:04,998 --> 00:11:06,626
especially Bradford Wilcox.

174
00:11:06,799 --> 00:11:07,823
Yeah, I know Brad.

175
00:11:08,001 --> 00:11:10,766
Then, you know he's nothing
like you. He's really... He's rigid.

176
00:11:10,937 --> 00:11:15,932
Didn't like, heh, the crepe
paper or anything else about me.

177
00:11:16,109 --> 00:11:18,840
Uh-huh. You're looking for work.

178
00:11:19,379 --> 00:11:22,008
Mr. Matlock, I'm an even better
lawyer than I was two years ago

179
00:11:22,182 --> 00:11:24,913
and I joined a tennis club. You
and I could go out on the weekends,

180
00:11:25,084 --> 00:11:27,644
hit the ball around,
a little cardiovascular.

181
00:11:30,623 --> 00:11:32,683
Yeah, I'm not much for tennis.

182
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,821
And I've got my
daughter working for me.

183
00:11:36,996 --> 00:11:39,932
We've got a young man

184
00:11:40,099 --> 00:11:43,160
and he's already complaining
he doesn't have enough to do.

185
00:11:43,570 --> 00:11:44,594
Yeah, I understand.

186
00:11:44,771 --> 00:11:46,103
And for what it's worth,

187
00:11:46,272 --> 00:11:50,004
I still don't have tickets to
the Braves and the Hawks.

188
00:12:28,214 --> 00:12:29,512
Brad.

189
00:12:31,551 --> 00:12:32,746
Brad, over here.

190
00:13:04,651 --> 00:13:06,176
What the...?

191
00:13:15,962 --> 00:13:18,431
Mr. McCormick, as a
member of the state bar,

192
00:13:18,598 --> 00:13:22,194
I know you are aware of the
severity of these charges against you.

193
00:13:23,603 --> 00:13:25,970
Will you please
approach the bench?

194
00:13:27,607 --> 00:13:30,372
- Sir.
- While you are within your right

195
00:13:30,543 --> 00:13:32,842
to represent yourself
against these charges,

196
00:13:33,012 --> 00:13:36,107
I feel I would be remiss in my
duties if I did not strongly advise you

197
00:13:36,282 --> 00:13:38,615
to seek more experienced
counsel for your defense.

198
00:13:39,118 --> 00:13:40,586
Thank you, Your Honor.

199
00:13:41,954 --> 00:13:44,617
I take it that means you intend
to remain your own attorney.

200
00:13:44,791 --> 00:13:47,192
I don't see as I have
much choice, Your Honor.

201
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:51,263
To be perfectly candid with you,
I've overextended myself a little bit.

202
00:13:51,431 --> 00:13:54,424
I'm not in a financial position
to hire one of the top firms,

203
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:57,001
and I have more
confidence in myself

204
00:13:57,170 --> 00:13:59,537
than a court-appointed attorney.

205
00:13:59,806 --> 00:14:01,206
All right.

206
00:14:01,374 --> 00:14:03,536
Then on to the subject of bail.

207
00:14:06,446 --> 00:14:07,505
- Turkey dog.
- Thank you.

208
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:09,114
You're welcome.

209
00:14:09,282 --> 00:14:11,046
- How can you eat that?
- Eat what?

210
00:14:11,217 --> 00:14:14,051
- Turkey hot dog.
- Tastes just like yours.

211
00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:15,552
Turkey. Does not.

212
00:14:15,722 --> 00:14:18,214
Yes, it does. And it
has less than half the fat.

213
00:14:18,391 --> 00:14:20,792
I don't care what you
call it. That's not a hot dog.

214
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:23,623
Roast beef made with
turkey is not roast beef.

215
00:14:23,796 --> 00:14:24,957
I'll remember that, Pop.

216
00:14:25,131 --> 00:14:27,225
Ben? Mr. Matlock?

217
00:14:28,601 --> 00:14:30,126
I take it you heard the news.

218
00:14:30,303 --> 00:14:33,933
Hard to miss. Meet my daughter,
Leanne. Todd McCormick.

219
00:14:34,107 --> 00:14:35,370
- Hello, Tom.
- Hi.

220
00:14:35,541 --> 00:14:36,804
Come on

221
00:14:37,377 --> 00:14:39,869
I hear they found
explosives in your garage.

222
00:14:40,046 --> 00:14:41,309
Ben, somebody is setting me up.

223
00:14:41,481 --> 00:14:44,246
I don't know the difference
between a firecracker and a grenade.

224
00:14:45,818 --> 00:14:48,014
Papers say he was caught
running from the motel.

225
00:14:48,187 --> 00:14:50,554
- I read that.
- You bet. Once Brad's car went up,

226
00:14:50,723 --> 00:14:53,852
it took me all of five seconds
to realize I'm being framed.

227
00:14:54,026 --> 00:14:55,050
Yeah.

228
00:14:55,228 --> 00:14:57,561
That morning, I went to
clean out my desk at the office.

229
00:14:57,730 --> 00:15:00,256
I found a message
supposedly written from Brad,

230
00:15:00,433 --> 00:15:03,267
asking me to meet him
at the Roadside Motel.

231
00:15:03,703 --> 00:15:05,296
It felt weird right
from the beginning.

232
00:15:05,471 --> 00:15:07,770
I never liked Brad, but
he's a straight-up guy.

233
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:10,000
A cryptic note like
that, not his style.

234
00:15:10,176 --> 00:15:12,145
- Still got the note?
- Gone.

235
00:15:13,713 --> 00:15:14,976
You know, the judge suggested

236
00:15:15,148 --> 00:15:18,482
that representing
myself isn't a great idea.

237
00:15:19,552 --> 00:15:20,576
Mm-hm.

238
00:15:20,753 --> 00:15:22,881
But I told him I couldn't
afford a good attorney.

239
00:15:23,055 --> 00:15:24,387
Hm.

240
00:15:27,493 --> 00:15:29,223
Would...? Does begging help?

241
00:15:37,837 --> 00:15:39,931
Maybe we should check in first.
- Fine.

242
00:15:40,106 --> 00:15:41,972
You don't think we
should check in?

243
00:15:43,743 --> 00:15:47,441
No. This was Mr. Wilcox's
first time at the Roadside Motel.

244
00:15:47,613 --> 00:15:48,706
Sure about that?

245
00:15:48,881 --> 00:15:51,976
- Well, we don't get a lot of suits here.
- Hmm.

246
00:16:03,996 --> 00:16:05,328
No Bible.

247
00:16:05,498 --> 00:16:08,297
Well, we don't get a lot
of readers here either.

248
00:16:09,202 --> 00:16:11,398
Mr. Wilcox paid you in cash?

249
00:16:11,571 --> 00:16:15,099
Yep. He said he wanted the
room for a couple of hours.

250
00:16:15,908 --> 00:16:17,467
Yeah?

251
00:16:17,643 --> 00:16:19,635
No, he didn't do that.

252
00:16:21,414 --> 00:16:24,407
- Didn't do what?
- What you're thinking.

253
00:16:26,285 --> 00:16:28,311
How do you know
what I'm thinking?

254
00:16:28,488 --> 00:16:30,855
Well, because there would be...

255
00:16:31,090 --> 00:16:32,888
How shall I put this?

256
00:16:33,059 --> 00:16:35,688
Telltale signs of things I'm
sure you don't wanna hear.

257
00:16:36,729 --> 00:16:37,753
Like what?

258
00:16:38,197 --> 00:16:41,725
Like a bed used, a shower used.

259
00:16:41,901 --> 00:16:43,494
Use your imagination.

260
00:16:43,669 --> 00:16:48,266
You mean, Mr. Wilcox
was in here by himself?

261
00:16:48,441 --> 00:16:50,569
Hey, come on. Do
I look like a spy?

262
00:16:50,743 --> 00:16:53,212
Privacy is our watchword
here at the Roadside.

263
00:16:53,379 --> 00:16:55,211
It's what made our reputation.

264
00:16:55,381 --> 00:16:57,782
I'm sure it isn't the furniture.

265
00:17:05,525 --> 00:17:09,895
Was Brad at all troubled
before his death?

266
00:17:10,196 --> 00:17:12,961
- You ever meet Brad, Miss Mclntyre?
- No.

267
00:17:13,132 --> 00:17:16,660
Oh. The reason I ask is that Brad
was, by nature, a serious person.

268
00:17:16,836 --> 00:17:19,533
He didn't socialize much.

269
00:17:19,705 --> 00:17:21,571
He didn't have much
of a sense of humor.

270
00:17:22,041 --> 00:17:24,875
So it would have been difficult to
tell if anything was bothering him.

271
00:17:25,044 --> 00:17:27,878
Well, I spent the last
few weeks with Brad

272
00:17:28,047 --> 00:17:30,983
and he seemed like
his same old self to me.

273
00:17:31,450 --> 00:17:33,942
So why do you think he
was resigning from the firm?

274
00:17:34,587 --> 00:17:35,714
What?

275
00:17:37,189 --> 00:17:40,318
Oh, my... Oh, you
didn't know about that?

276
00:17:40,493 --> 00:17:42,655
No. LEANNE: Well, yes.

277
00:17:42,828 --> 00:17:45,195
The police found a
letter of resignation

278
00:17:45,364 --> 00:17:48,163
on Brad's desk at his house.

279
00:17:50,603 --> 00:17:51,764
I can't believe this.

280
00:17:51,938 --> 00:17:54,965
Of course, a handwriting
expert will have to determine

281
00:17:55,141 --> 00:17:57,042
whether he actually wrote it.

282
00:17:57,743 --> 00:18:00,303
I guess we didn't know
Brad as well as we thought.

283
00:18:02,214 --> 00:18:07,050
- Wow, this is so weird.
- What's that?

284
00:18:08,688 --> 00:18:13,183
Well, I mean, why would
Brad resign from this firm

285
00:18:13,359 --> 00:18:17,057
when he's in the middle of one
of the biggest cases he'd ever tried

286
00:18:17,229 --> 00:18:19,630
and not tell his
partners about it?

287
00:18:52,832 --> 00:18:55,859
- I'm exhausted.
- Mm.

288
00:18:56,035 --> 00:18:58,630
Just another six or seven
hours and I'll let you go.

289
00:18:58,804 --> 00:19:01,467
Mm. Heh, heh, heh.

290
00:19:13,185 --> 00:19:15,484
The whole idea
makes no sense to me.

291
00:19:15,655 --> 00:19:18,181
Gavin $ Howard will
never hire me as a lawyer.

292
00:19:18,357 --> 00:19:21,919
- I promise they will. You got any fours?
- Go fish.

293
00:19:22,094 --> 00:19:24,188
Ben, I went to law
school in Mount Harlan.

294
00:19:24,363 --> 00:19:26,696
It took me forever to
pass the bar. Remember?

295
00:19:26,866 --> 00:19:29,563
- Not a problem.
- What do you mean?

296
00:19:29,735 --> 00:19:30,794
Got any kings?

297
00:19:31,370 --> 00:19:34,807
I asked you a minute
ago if you had any kings.

298
00:19:34,974 --> 00:19:37,375
I didn't have any
kings a minute ago.

299
00:19:41,647 --> 00:19:43,912
Ooh! Hoo-hoo-hoo.

300
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:49,147
- What's this?
- Read.

301
00:19:49,321 --> 00:19:50,345
Read.

302
00:19:53,225 --> 00:19:54,955
Criminy.

303
00:19:55,227 --> 00:19:57,594
A letter of recommendation
from Cosgrove $ O'Malley.

304
00:19:57,763 --> 00:19:59,095
They're the top
firm in New York.

305
00:20:00,032 --> 00:20:02,627
You were their
favorite associate.

306
00:20:02,802 --> 00:20:05,294
Ben, I've been to New York City

307
00:20:05,471 --> 00:20:08,134
a total of two days in my entire
life for my Aunt Bessie's funeral.

308
00:20:08,307 --> 00:20:10,833
Why are they
recommending me for this job?

309
00:20:11,010 --> 00:20:13,980
- I asked.
- Oh.

310
00:20:14,146 --> 00:20:17,639
Russ Cosgrove is one of
my oldest and dearest friends.

311
00:20:17,817 --> 00:20:20,844
I promised him you
wouldn't embarrass them.

312
00:20:21,387 --> 00:20:23,549
Got any sevens?

313
00:20:24,223 --> 00:20:26,488
When did you start
getting sevens?

314
00:20:46,912 --> 00:20:50,246
We're not gonna play games with
you, Cliff. That's not who we are.

315
00:20:50,416 --> 00:20:51,907
But we'd be lying
to you if we said

316
00:20:52,084 --> 00:20:54,644
we didn't want you as
a member of this firm.

317
00:20:55,020 --> 00:20:57,854
I'm, uh... I'm very
flattered. Thank you.

318
00:20:58,023 --> 00:21:00,754
I think you'll find our offer
more than competitive.

319
00:21:00,926 --> 00:21:03,259
I expect you're
speaking with other firms.

320
00:21:03,729 --> 00:21:08,326
Um... Yes. Only the major ones,
though. It's a very exhausting process.

321
00:21:08,501 --> 00:21:12,438
- Heh, heh, heh. I'll bet.
- Clifford, take whatever time you need.

322
00:21:14,406 --> 00:21:16,739
But know this. We want you.

323
00:21:23,315 --> 00:21:25,841
Mr. Howard, given your
associate's untimely death,

324
00:21:26,018 --> 00:21:27,919
I assumed you'd
ask for a mistrial.

325
00:21:28,087 --> 00:21:30,113
Your Honor, it is my
clients' understanding

326
00:21:30,289 --> 00:21:32,724
that a mistrial would only
increase their legal costs

327
00:21:32,892 --> 00:21:35,384
and prolong this
action unnecessarily.

328
00:21:35,561 --> 00:21:38,588
- We're prepared to continue this trial.
- I see.

329
00:21:38,931 --> 00:21:40,559
Have you any
objection, Mr. Canon?

330
00:21:40,733 --> 00:21:43,053
Your Honor, I've discussed
this at some length with counsel

331
00:21:43,135 --> 00:21:44,865
and I have no objection.

332
00:21:45,037 --> 00:21:47,632
Then this court will
reconvene tomorrow 10 a.m.

333
00:21:57,249 --> 00:22:00,083
I'm going to send that judge a
present for adjourning us early.

334
00:22:02,154 --> 00:22:04,817
- It's good to see you.
- I ordered for you.

335
00:22:04,990 --> 00:22:06,481
Thank you.

336
00:22:06,659 --> 00:22:09,128
- So how's the case going?
- Who cares?

337
00:22:09,295 --> 00:22:10,820
No, really, I'm interested.

338
00:22:12,531 --> 00:22:16,161
You know I'm not supposed
to talk about the case.

339
00:22:16,335 --> 00:22:19,203
- Heh, heh, heh.
- Like you're gonna tell someone.

340
00:22:19,371 --> 00:22:22,398
Well, I remember, you told
me about it the other day.

341
00:22:22,575 --> 00:22:25,670
So have you decided
how you're gonna vote?

342
00:22:25,845 --> 00:22:29,213
I'm not supposed to make up
my mind until all the evidence is in.

343
00:22:29,381 --> 00:22:32,215
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Heh, heh, heh.

344
00:22:33,986 --> 00:22:38,082
These fat cats from Andros
Chemical are so guilty, it's disgusting.

345
00:22:38,257 --> 00:22:40,453
I mean, their chemicals
are killing all these people.

346
00:22:40,626 --> 00:22:43,653
They're not even
copping to it. I...

347
00:22:43,829 --> 00:22:45,491
I hope we put them
out of business.

348
00:22:45,664 --> 00:22:48,725
Well, I hear that Andros Chemical
is a very powerful company.

349
00:22:48,901 --> 00:22:50,665
Well, that ain't gonna
save them this time.

350
00:22:50,836 --> 00:22:54,432
Yeah. Yeah. But companies
like Andros can play pretty dirty.

351
00:22:54,607 --> 00:22:57,133
And how do you know so
much about Andros Chemical?

352
00:22:57,309 --> 00:22:59,744
- What, do you own stock?
- Heh, heh, heh.

353
00:23:00,913 --> 00:23:03,906
They know about
you and me, Gary.

354
00:23:05,417 --> 00:23:06,885
What...?

355
00:23:13,826 --> 00:23:15,590
Oh, my God.

356
00:23:18,631 --> 00:23:20,259
You work for them.

357
00:23:20,432 --> 00:23:24,028
They know your wife doesn't
like you fooling around.

358
00:23:24,203 --> 00:23:27,435
- Christy, please. Please don't do this.
- It's not up to me, Gary.

359
00:23:27,606 --> 00:23:29,199
Christy.

360
00:23:31,176 --> 00:23:34,578
Janet will divorce me if
she sees those pictures.

361
00:23:36,682 --> 00:23:39,846
Then I suggest you
convince your 11 friends

362
00:23:40,019 --> 00:23:45,754
that Andros Chemical
pay very little damages.

363
00:23:58,871 --> 00:24:02,740
Aw, it's a beautiful
day. A beautiful day.

364
00:24:13,118 --> 00:24:16,714
Dad, Dad, Dad. Come on,
everybody's looking at you.

365
00:24:16,889 --> 00:24:18,255
They think you're nuts.

366
00:24:18,424 --> 00:24:20,904
Hello, young gentleman. How's
the wife, especially your mother?

367
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:22,221
Dad. Dad, come on.

368
00:24:22,394 --> 00:24:24,829
Now, we have to be back at
the judge's chambers at 2:30.

369
00:24:24,997 --> 00:24:26,037
What do you want? Chinese?

370
00:24:26,198 --> 00:24:29,100
I don't know. Chinese
is always giving me...

371
00:24:29,268 --> 00:24:32,500
How about Chinese?
Well, I'll be...

372
00:24:51,523 --> 00:24:54,254
- Whose car?
- Mine. They gave it to me.

373
00:24:54,426 --> 00:24:56,418
They let me pick
out my own color.

374
00:24:56,595 --> 00:24:58,928
Wow, it's beautiful.

375
00:24:59,098 --> 00:25:01,499
Oh, and they gave me
my own expense account.

376
00:25:01,667 --> 00:25:03,932
I never had an expense account.

377
00:25:04,103 --> 00:25:06,663
Those big firms, they do
stuff like that all the time.

378
00:25:06,839 --> 00:25:08,467
And they gave me a
corporate credit card.

379
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:11,041
Tomorrow we'll do
lunch. I'll pick you up.

380
00:25:11,210 --> 00:25:13,577
Do all the associates get a car?

381
00:25:13,746 --> 00:25:15,942
Oh, I don't know. I've...

382
00:25:16,115 --> 00:25:18,141
I think they really wanted me.

383
00:25:18,317 --> 00:25:21,515
Because he isn't
who they think he is.

384
00:25:21,687 --> 00:25:26,455
Well, you know, they think he's
from some big firm. Remember?

385
00:25:27,726 --> 00:25:31,993
I bet that they pay the associates
more than you pay Leanne.

386
00:25:32,931 --> 00:25:34,297
They do?

387
00:25:34,466 --> 00:25:36,560
Everything is not about money.

388
00:25:38,003 --> 00:25:40,234
- How much do they pay you?
- Hoo. Heh.

389
00:25:40,406 --> 00:25:42,068
I'm going to lunch.

390
00:25:42,241 --> 00:25:45,109
They pay for my
car insurance too.

391
00:25:48,213 --> 00:25:49,545
<i>Yeah.</i>

392
00:25:51,116 --> 00:25:53,436
You'll be assisting me on
the Andros Chemical case.

393
00:25:53,519 --> 00:25:56,011
These are the original
deposition of the homeowners,

394
00:25:56,188 --> 00:25:58,748
medical reports of
the cancer victims,

395
00:25:58,924 --> 00:26:02,326
chemical analysis of the
down plant subsoil and water.

396
00:26:02,494 --> 00:26:05,931
Your predecessor was collecting
similar geological samples,

397
00:26:06,098 --> 00:26:09,159
so we can compare
cancer incidences.

398
00:26:10,169 --> 00:26:12,695
- Geology's not really my thing.
- Make it your thing.

399
00:26:12,871 --> 00:26:16,000
I'll need your conclusions for
court on Monday. Any questions?

400
00:26:16,175 --> 00:26:18,371
Ahem, yes. As a matter of
fact, I do have a question.

401
00:26:18,544 --> 00:26:20,740
I've been following this
case in the newspaper.

402
00:26:20,913 --> 00:26:22,833
I was wondering why
you didn't ask for a mistrial

403
00:26:22,981 --> 00:26:24,415
when Bradford Wilcox died.

404
00:26:24,583 --> 00:26:27,644
Brad did a great job choosing
the jury. I didn't wanna lose them.

405
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:31,052
My office is right down
the hall if you need me.

406
00:26:31,423 --> 00:26:32,516
Good luck.

407
00:26:33,058 --> 00:26:34,617
Thank you.

408
00:26:37,362 --> 00:26:39,058
Thank you.

409
00:26:54,046 --> 00:26:55,639
Excuse me.

410
00:26:56,915 --> 00:26:58,645
- Hey. Hey, you must be Cliff Lewis.
- Yeah.

411
00:26:58,817 --> 00:27:00,547
Yeah, Rob Norris.
I've heard about you.

412
00:27:00,719 --> 00:27:03,211
Hi. Look, do me a favor. Give
me a call and we'll go to lunch.

413
00:27:03,388 --> 00:27:06,290
I'll give you the skinny
on everyone here, okay?

414
00:27:39,191 --> 00:27:41,387
Thank you, sir. See
you this afternoon.

415
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:43,859
Hi. How you doing?

416
00:28:16,695 --> 00:28:18,425
Hello, Cliff.

417
00:28:19,831 --> 00:28:23,165
- Hello.
- Burning the midnight oil already, huh?

418
00:28:23,335 --> 00:28:26,931
Yes, actually, I'm almost
done. Can I help you?

419
00:28:27,105 --> 00:28:30,371
Oh, no, no, no. I like
to do my own research.

420
00:28:30,542 --> 00:28:35,640
That way I've got it all right up here
when I go into the courtroom. Heh, heh.

421
00:28:35,814 --> 00:28:39,046
Well, how's it going?

422
00:28:39,218 --> 00:28:40,584
Pretty good. Pretty good.

423
00:28:40,752 --> 00:28:44,917
I'm, uh, a little slow out of the
starting gate, but I'm picking up speed.

424
00:28:45,090 --> 00:28:48,788
Good. Good. I like to hear that.

425
00:28:52,731 --> 00:28:55,360
Well, have a nice night.

426
00:28:55,534 --> 00:28:58,299
Heh, heh. I'll try.

427
00:29:12,618 --> 00:29:15,986
Hi, don't you ever
go home? It's late.

428
00:29:16,154 --> 00:29:18,123
No, no, I like to do
my own research.

429
00:29:18,290 --> 00:29:20,657
That way when I go
into court, it's right here.

430
00:29:20,826 --> 00:29:23,091
- I'm Linda Maxwell.
- Oh, Cliff Lewis.

431
00:29:23,262 --> 00:29:26,323
I know. I saw you when
you were interviewing.

432
00:29:26,498 --> 00:29:28,490
I thought you're
very attractive.

433
00:29:28,667 --> 00:29:31,159
But it seemed premature
to say something then.

434
00:29:32,838 --> 00:29:35,967
Oh. Oh, ooh. Yeah, I'm
trying to get some work done.

435
00:29:36,141 --> 00:29:37,609
Could we discuss this tomorrow?

436
00:29:37,776 --> 00:29:38,903
Am I making you nervous?

437
00:29:39,077 --> 00:29:40,705
Oh, no. No, not at all.

438
00:29:41,346 --> 00:29:43,372
Well, I thought now that
you've joined the firm,

439
00:29:43,548 --> 00:29:45,483
maybe we could have dinner.

440
00:29:46,151 --> 00:29:48,677
Oh, yeah, that would
be great. Um... Uh...

441
00:29:49,087 --> 00:29:51,022
I'll check my calendar.
How about next week?

442
00:29:51,290 --> 00:29:53,020
- Am I being too forward?
- No.

443
00:29:53,191 --> 00:29:55,251
I don't believe in standing
on ceremony, do you?

444
00:29:55,427 --> 00:30:00,263
No, no, no. I don't mind if
women wanna ask me out.

445
00:30:00,432 --> 00:30:01,552
Actually, I kind of like it.

446
00:30:01,867 --> 00:30:03,529
Does it happen that often?

447
00:30:04,436 --> 00:30:07,964
No. No. What I meant was

448
00:30:08,140 --> 00:30:10,837
that I would love to
talk to you about dinner.

449
00:30:11,009 --> 00:30:13,672
Why don't we... Why
don't we do that tomorrow?

450
00:30:13,845 --> 00:30:14,869
- Deal.
- Deal.

451
00:30:15,047 --> 00:30:16,481
Tomorrow.

452
00:30:42,574 --> 00:30:44,270
Richard?

453
00:30:46,678 --> 00:30:48,203
Richard?

454
00:30:54,086 --> 00:30:55,166
Who is that woman?

455
00:30:55,287 --> 00:30:57,518
I don't know. I don't
know who he is either.

456
00:30:58,056 --> 00:31:00,582
Well, why would they
shred a picture like that?

457
00:31:00,759 --> 00:31:02,489
Blackmail.

458
00:31:03,962 --> 00:31:05,760
Yeah. Maybe.

459
00:31:05,931 --> 00:31:10,164
You see that table? I'd
recognize that table anywhere.

460
00:31:27,018 --> 00:31:28,919
Oh, say, Bert, how you doing?

461
00:31:29,087 --> 00:31:30,919
What the hell do you want?

462
00:31:31,089 --> 00:31:32,284
Who's the girl?

463
00:31:32,457 --> 00:31:33,755
How should I know?

464
00:31:34,059 --> 00:31:37,029
I figured this picture's got to have
been made in one of your rooms.

465
00:31:37,195 --> 00:31:39,790
Nobody who's ever seen
these tables will ever forget them,

466
00:31:39,965 --> 00:31:42,867
especially the one with the
Indian on it. What's her name?

467
00:31:43,168 --> 00:31:45,433
Girls like that don't pay.
I never got her name.

468
00:31:45,604 --> 00:31:47,732
What about the guy?
One of them paid.

469
00:31:47,906 --> 00:31:49,169
I don't remember.

470
00:31:50,075 --> 00:31:54,012
I figure this picture had to have
been made from another room.

471
00:31:54,179 --> 00:31:56,842
Maybe as a set-up for
blackmail or something like that.

472
00:31:57,015 --> 00:32:00,015
Hey, wait a minute. Wait. I resent
that immensely. Like I told you before...

473
00:32:00,185 --> 00:32:01,847
Privacy is your birthmark.

474
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:03,249
No, no, my watchword.

475
00:32:03,422 --> 00:32:06,392
Now, why don't you
get the hell out of here.

476
00:32:07,225 --> 00:32:10,923
I bet when people check in,
they park right here in front.

477
00:32:11,096 --> 00:32:12,758
I said get lost.

478
00:32:12,931 --> 00:32:19,337
And I bet that that camera is
there to video cars and people,

479
00:32:19,504 --> 00:32:22,872
so that you can identify them if
they give you the wrong name.

480
00:32:23,041 --> 00:32:24,976
- You're very smart.
- Yeah.

481
00:32:25,143 --> 00:32:27,578
You know, I could
subpoena those tapes.

482
00:32:27,746 --> 00:32:29,612
But you don't want to
have cops around here.

483
00:32:29,781 --> 00:32:33,582
So why don't you
just give them to me.

484
00:32:39,090 --> 00:32:41,525
Hard to believe Cliff's
making more money than me.

485
00:32:41,693 --> 00:32:43,773
Yeah, it's hard to
believe. It's astounding.

486
00:32:43,962 --> 00:32:45,897
He has a car.

487
00:32:46,298 --> 00:32:48,927
- You have a car.
- That I paid for.

488
00:32:49,100 --> 00:32:52,434
I mean, I've been a lawyer at
least 10 years longer than Cliff.

489
00:32:52,604 --> 00:32:55,472
Well, Leanne, you wouldn't
like it in one of those big firms.

490
00:32:55,640 --> 00:32:57,108
Here. It's coming up.

491
00:32:57,275 --> 00:32:58,834
I don't have an expense account.

492
00:32:59,010 --> 00:33:02,105
Oh, I take you to
lunch all the time.

493
00:33:02,280 --> 00:33:05,648
See. It's that guy there
with the chain on his glasses.

494
00:33:05,817 --> 00:33:07,285
He's the one in the picture.

495
00:33:08,787 --> 00:33:11,689
Dad, I'd like a raise.

496
00:33:12,424 --> 00:33:13,949
You just got one.

497
00:33:14,125 --> 00:33:16,685
Dad, come on. I
mean, I should at least

498
00:33:16,862 --> 00:33:19,696
be making as much money
as they're paying Cliff.

499
00:33:19,865 --> 00:33:21,766
Leanne, I can't afford that.

500
00:33:21,933 --> 00:33:23,162
Yes, you can afford it.

501
00:33:23,335 --> 00:33:25,236
Leanne. I can't afford that.

502
00:33:25,403 --> 00:33:27,235
I do the books. I know
what you can afford.

503
00:33:27,405 --> 00:33:29,874
- I'm not made of money.
- Dad.

504
00:33:30,041 --> 00:33:32,670
What if people quit hiring us?

505
00:33:32,844 --> 00:33:34,506
Well, you'll fire me.

506
00:33:34,679 --> 00:33:37,239
I can't fire my own daughter.

507
00:33:37,415 --> 00:33:39,145
I'm taking a raise.

508
00:33:39,317 --> 00:33:42,549
- Can we get back to the case?
- I'm taking a raise.

509
00:33:42,721 --> 00:33:46,055
Okay. Well, you can't see the
license plate on that man's car.

510
00:33:46,224 --> 00:33:48,819
I know. I took the tape
and I had it enlarged

511
00:33:48,994 --> 00:33:50,724
so we could see it
better. Here it comes.

512
00:33:50,896 --> 00:33:52,330
It's... There, there.

513
00:33:52,497 --> 00:33:55,934
It's a blue Chevy with a
dent on the front fender.

514
00:33:56,701 --> 00:33:58,932
Pause the tape. Pause the tape.

515
00:34:01,640 --> 00:34:03,768
There's something
on the windshield.

516
00:34:04,576 --> 00:34:07,341
That's from the county
court's parking lot.

517
00:34:07,846 --> 00:34:09,542
Let's go.

518
00:34:10,048 --> 00:34:12,574
I guess I earned my raise.

519
00:34:20,792 --> 00:34:24,024
Jaguar, Oldsmobile.

520
00:34:24,863 --> 00:34:25,922
Yeah.

521
00:34:26,097 --> 00:34:30,330
Ford, Ford. Ford.

522
00:34:30,802 --> 00:34:32,395
Chevy.

523
00:34:36,274 --> 00:34:37,902
Leanne.

524
00:34:39,311 --> 00:34:40,836
He's here.

525
00:34:41,012 --> 00:34:42,446
- Let's go.
- Where we going?

526
00:34:42,614 --> 00:34:46,483
This has got to have something to
do with the Andros Chemical case.

527
00:34:47,285 --> 00:34:48,365
First place to go and look.

528
00:34:48,520 --> 00:34:51,615
Okay. All right. If we don't find
him, I'll run a make on the plates

529
00:34:51,790 --> 00:34:55,056
- and see who owns the car.
- Okay.

530
00:35:11,710 --> 00:35:13,872
- Don't see him.
- Mm.

531
00:35:30,028 --> 00:35:31,690
- What are you doing here?
- Get away.

532
00:35:31,863 --> 00:35:33,832
- What?
- Get away from us.

533
00:35:50,915 --> 00:35:53,384
All rise.

534
00:35:54,285 --> 00:35:56,413
Mr. Howard, Mr. Canon,

535
00:35:56,588 --> 00:35:59,558
I will see you in my
chambers this minute.

536
00:36:08,233 --> 00:36:11,101
It appears we have a
case of jury tampering here.

537
00:36:11,269 --> 00:36:13,465
Now, Mr. Adler, who
is juror number nine,

538
00:36:13,838 --> 00:36:16,103
has admitted to me that
he's being blackmailed

539
00:36:16,274 --> 00:36:19,472
to ask for the lowest
possible damages in the case.

540
00:36:21,913 --> 00:36:24,405
- Have you seen this picture before?
- No, of course not.

541
00:36:24,582 --> 00:36:26,902
Your Honor, if those pictures
come out, my marriage is over

542
00:36:27,052 --> 00:36:28,281
and I love my wife.

543
00:36:28,453 --> 00:36:29,944
Then you shouldn't
have had an affair.

544
00:36:30,121 --> 00:36:32,716
I don't think they'll be
showing the pictures again.

545
00:36:32,891 --> 00:36:35,053
James, do you know
anything about this?

546
00:36:35,226 --> 00:36:36,888
Your Honor, I already said no.

547
00:36:37,062 --> 00:36:38,655
Very well.

548
00:36:39,864 --> 00:36:42,527
There will be an exhaustive
investigation into this incident.

549
00:36:42,701 --> 00:36:45,000
Or more specifically,
James, it will be into you,

550
00:36:45,370 --> 00:36:47,464
your law firm, your client.

551
00:36:47,639 --> 00:36:50,040
It sure would help if we
knew the name of that woman.

552
00:36:50,208 --> 00:36:52,143
Um, her name is Christy Elkins.

553
00:36:52,310 --> 00:36:53,630
And where will we find her?

554
00:36:53,778 --> 00:36:56,418
I've been trying to reach her
for days. I think that she left town.

555
00:36:56,581 --> 00:36:58,209
We're going back into court.

556
00:36:58,383 --> 00:37:00,682
I'm dismissing the
jury, declaring a mistrial.

557
00:37:01,319 --> 00:37:05,256
Then we'll bring in the police.

558
00:37:12,263 --> 00:37:14,642
Look, I don't know what
you're talking about, all right?

559
00:37:14,666 --> 00:37:17,101
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

560
00:37:24,442 --> 00:37:27,879
You know, even if Richard Gavin
or James Allen Howard were here,

561
00:37:28,046 --> 00:37:29,639
he'd never tell you.

562
00:37:29,814 --> 00:37:32,943
I know, but I'm
right about this.

563
00:37:33,118 --> 00:37:36,213
Brad Wilcox left his
room and started his car

564
00:37:36,387 --> 00:37:38,788
at the exact moment
you showed up.

565
00:37:38,957 --> 00:37:40,550
That's too much
of a coincidence.

566
00:37:40,725 --> 00:37:45,356
Somebody had to have been watching
and called the motel when you arrived.

567
00:37:46,097 --> 00:37:50,091
You tell me where
he called from.

568
00:38:13,324 --> 00:38:14,348
Hi.

569
00:38:14,526 --> 00:38:17,018
- What will it be?
- Bottle of pop.

570
00:38:17,195 --> 00:38:19,824
I'll have a bottle of sparkling
mineral water, please.

571
00:38:19,998 --> 00:38:21,432
Mineral what?

572
00:38:21,599 --> 00:38:23,124
Two bottles of pop.

573
00:38:23,301 --> 00:38:25,099
Help yourself.

574
00:38:29,607 --> 00:38:31,200
Not much business, huh?

575
00:38:31,376 --> 00:38:34,471
Mister, we get three
customers in here, it's a crowd.

576
00:38:35,013 --> 00:38:38,040
Ever since they put the
interstate in, business up and died.

577
00:38:38,216 --> 00:38:41,152
- You've been here long?
- A couple of years.

578
00:38:42,220 --> 00:38:44,416
Were you here the day
the car blew up at the motel?

579
00:38:44,589 --> 00:38:46,114
Yeah.

580
00:38:46,925 --> 00:38:49,918
A perfect view of the motel.
- Hmm. Um...

581
00:38:50,161 --> 00:38:51,629
Look at this picture.

582
00:38:51,796 --> 00:38:55,563
See if you recognize
either one of these men.

583
00:38:56,401 --> 00:38:58,233
Oh...

584
00:38:58,403 --> 00:39:03,307
I couldn't say. Why, you cops?
- Mm-hm.

585
00:39:06,244 --> 00:39:09,009
That's the only telephone?
- Uh-huh.

586
00:39:09,180 --> 00:39:10,808
Hmm.

587
00:39:12,183 --> 00:39:13,583
What?

588
00:39:17,622 --> 00:39:18,920
I like that look.

589
00:39:19,357 --> 00:39:22,020
I don't know what it
means, but I like that look.

590
00:39:25,563 --> 00:39:27,498
On the afternoon
that he was murdered,

591
00:39:27,665 --> 00:39:30,794
Bradford Wilcox was
representing Andros Chemical

592
00:39:30,969 --> 00:39:32,870
in a very large
lawsuit, wasn't he?

593
00:39:33,037 --> 00:39:34,596
Yes.

594
00:39:34,839 --> 00:39:38,708
What kind of damages do you
think the plaintiff can expect?

595
00:39:39,978 --> 00:39:44,848
Haven't damages in cases
like this in the past run,

596
00:39:45,016 --> 00:39:47,918
oh, as high as $50 million?

597
00:39:48,086 --> 00:39:49,247
Yes, that's true.

598
00:39:49,420 --> 00:39:54,085
So, what's your firm's
standing with Andros Chemical?

599
00:39:54,626 --> 00:39:59,223
Excellent. We've represented
them for more than 15 years.

600
00:40:01,299 --> 00:40:08,672
But haven't they recently moved
some of their work to another firm?

601
00:40:09,207 --> 00:40:11,699
Maritime work, which
we really don't handle.

602
00:40:15,880 --> 00:40:17,212
Ah.

603
00:40:17,382 --> 00:40:21,285
Well, didn't both you
and Richard Gavin

604
00:40:21,452 --> 00:40:26,891
believe that if the damages
went too high in this case, uh,

605
00:40:27,392 --> 00:40:31,261
you might lose Andros
Chemical as a client?

606
00:40:32,230 --> 00:40:34,426
Absolutely not.

607
00:40:36,200 --> 00:40:40,365
What's the standing
of that trial now?

608
00:40:40,538 --> 00:40:43,133
The judge declared a mistrial.

609
00:40:43,308 --> 00:40:44,503
On what grounds?

610
00:40:44,876 --> 00:40:47,573
An investigation of jury
tampering is underway.

611
00:40:47,745 --> 00:40:50,806
One of the jurors said he's
being blackmailed, didn't he?

612
00:40:51,082 --> 00:40:54,644
He was told that
compromising pictures of him

613
00:40:54,819 --> 00:40:56,845
and another woman
would be sent to his wife,

614
00:40:57,021 --> 00:40:59,047
unless he convinced
his fellow jurors

615
00:40:59,223 --> 00:41:04,662
to lowball the damages
in this case, huh?

616
00:41:04,829 --> 00:41:06,127
That's the allegation.

617
00:41:06,297 --> 00:41:08,357
Yeah. Who would benefit?

618
00:41:08,733 --> 00:41:10,292
Andros Chemical.

619
00:41:10,468 --> 00:41:13,700
Hmm. So if you and
Richard Gavin believed

620
00:41:13,871 --> 00:41:17,672
that you were in danger
of losing Andros Chemical,

621
00:41:17,842 --> 00:41:20,209
if you could come up with a way

622
00:41:20,378 --> 00:41:24,247
to get those damages down
as low as you possibly could,

623
00:41:24,415 --> 00:41:26,509
why, your firm would
benefit too, wouldn't it?

624
00:41:26,684 --> 00:41:29,916
If we were in danger of losing
their business, which we are not,

625
00:41:30,088 --> 00:41:33,547
- that would be true, I suppose.
- Uh...

626
00:41:34,225 --> 00:41:38,560
You and, uh, Bradford
Wilcox were partners.

627
00:41:38,730 --> 00:41:40,699
I guess you knew
him pretty well.

628
00:41:40,865 --> 00:41:44,802
So how do you suppose
he would have reacted

629
00:41:44,969 --> 00:41:47,905
if he found out somebody
was messing with the jury

630
00:41:48,072 --> 00:41:49,165
on a case he was trying?

631
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:52,003
Objection, Your Honor.
Calls for speculation.

632
00:41:52,176 --> 00:41:55,237
Bradford Wilcox and
James Allen Howard

633
00:41:55,413 --> 00:41:57,245
knew one another for 24 years.

634
00:41:57,415 --> 00:41:59,475
They were partners,
for God's sake.

635
00:42:00,284 --> 00:42:01,308
Witness will answer.

636
00:42:01,486 --> 00:42:05,617
If Brad knew that someone
was tampering with his jury,

637
00:42:05,790 --> 00:42:07,816
he would have gone
straight to the judge.

638
00:42:08,192 --> 00:42:09,524
No matter who was involved.

639
00:42:09,694 --> 00:42:12,323
Brad Wilcox was the most
honest person I've ever known.

640
00:42:12,497 --> 00:42:15,524
He wouldn't have let his own mother
get away with tampering with the jury.

641
00:42:15,700 --> 00:42:18,727
That's what everybody
says about Bradford Wilcox.

642
00:42:18,903 --> 00:42:22,931
That he was an honest,
moral, upstanding man,

643
00:42:23,107 --> 00:42:25,042
and that's why I can't...

644
00:42:25,209 --> 00:42:26,905
It's just hard to understand

645
00:42:27,078 --> 00:42:31,448
why he was at the Roadside
Motel the afternoon he was killed.

646
00:42:31,816 --> 00:42:34,251
Just... I just... You know what?

647
00:42:34,419 --> 00:42:36,513
Maybe he was there
to meet somebody.

648
00:42:37,422 --> 00:42:40,449
Probably this Christy Elkins,

649
00:42:41,592 --> 00:42:44,187
who was gonna confirm...

650
00:42:45,163 --> 00:42:47,325
his belief,

651
00:42:47,498 --> 00:42:50,593
suspicion that you were
the one who set that juror up.

652
00:42:50,768 --> 00:42:52,031
- What?
- Objection.

653
00:42:52,203 --> 00:42:54,123
- Mr. Matlock...
- He was gonna turn you in.

654
00:42:54,172 --> 00:42:57,199
And then you set him up
with the girl and then killed him.

655
00:42:57,775 --> 00:42:58,799
Don't!

656
00:42:58,976 --> 00:43:00,444
Mr. Matlock.

657
00:43:01,379 --> 00:43:02,403
I have proof.

658
00:43:02,580 --> 00:43:04,674
- Well, then get to it.
- All right.

659
00:43:07,385 --> 00:43:11,720
Howard, have you ever been
to the, uh, Roadside Motel?

660
00:43:11,889 --> 00:43:13,323
No.

661
00:43:13,791 --> 00:43:17,250
Roadside Motel, that's where
Bradford Wilcox was killed.

662
00:43:17,428 --> 00:43:19,148
- You sure you never been there?
- Positive.

663
00:43:19,297 --> 00:43:22,199
Ever been to the gas
station across the road?

664
00:43:22,366 --> 00:43:25,366
I told you I've never been to the
motel. I've never been to the gas station.

665
00:43:25,503 --> 00:43:28,962
So you were never at
the motel on Route 6

666
00:43:29,140 --> 00:43:30,733
or the gas station
across the road?

667
00:43:30,908 --> 00:43:35,175
How many times I have to
repeat this? I was not there. No.

668
00:43:36,581 --> 00:43:40,313
Well, the reason I keep
asking the question is I...

669
00:43:40,651 --> 00:43:43,143
I just... I just...

670
00:43:43,387 --> 00:43:48,257
I don't understand how
my client was framed.

671
00:43:48,659 --> 00:43:54,189
I mean, why did Mr. Wilcox
leave his room and go to his car

672
00:43:54,699 --> 00:43:58,693
the instant that
my client drove up?

673
00:43:59,237 --> 00:44:03,902
Well, the best answer, of course,
is that somebody was watching

674
00:44:04,075 --> 00:44:08,570
and called the manager of the motel
to deliver the message to Mr. Wilcox

675
00:44:08,746 --> 00:44:13,116
that the girl he was waiting
for was not gonna show up.

676
00:44:13,417 --> 00:44:16,979
I always suspected it
was you or Richard Gavin.

677
00:44:17,155 --> 00:44:19,624
I showed your pictures to
the manager of the motel

678
00:44:19,790 --> 00:44:21,418
and the people
at the gas station.

679
00:44:21,592 --> 00:44:23,584
They didn't recognize
you. I don't know why.

680
00:44:23,761 --> 00:44:26,629
- That's because I wasn't there.
- And I checked your phone bills.

681
00:44:26,797 --> 00:44:30,029
I mean, in your car, your credit
card, your house, your office.

682
00:44:30,201 --> 00:44:33,228
No calls to the Roadside Motel.

683
00:44:34,405 --> 00:44:37,273
I can't figure it out. I just...

684
00:44:37,441 --> 00:44:41,776
It beats me. I mean,
dead, it beat me.

685
00:44:42,213 --> 00:44:46,708
Till I went over to the gas
station, started looking around.

686
00:44:50,388 --> 00:44:53,916
They do very little business
over there, very little.

687
00:44:54,091 --> 00:44:58,153
They got one pay
telephone, heh, heh, heh,

688
00:44:58,329 --> 00:44:59,820
that's used so seldom

689
00:44:59,997 --> 00:45:03,195
they take the change out
of it every three months.

690
00:45:03,367 --> 00:45:05,097
Ha, ha, ha. Take
the change out...

691
00:45:05,269 --> 00:45:07,329
Take the change out
every three months.

692
00:45:07,505 --> 00:45:10,498
- Heh, heh, heh.
- Yeah, let's see. Where is it?

693
00:45:10,675 --> 00:45:14,112
Where is it? Oh, here,
thank you. Thank you.

694
00:45:14,645 --> 00:45:18,548
The Atlanta police
removed this quarter

695
00:45:18,783 --> 00:45:22,515
from that pay
telephone, Mr. Howard.

696
00:45:23,354 --> 00:45:26,950
Your fingerprints
are on both sides of it.

697
00:45:28,259 --> 00:45:30,455
You were at the Roadside Motel

698
00:45:30,628 --> 00:45:33,962
the afternoon
Mr. Wilcox was murdered.

699
00:45:35,967 --> 00:45:39,597
You paid somebody
to rig his car.

700
00:45:40,504 --> 00:45:42,871
And then when my
client showed up,

701
00:45:43,040 --> 00:45:48,172
you called the manager to relay
that message to him about that girl.

702
00:45:48,346 --> 00:45:52,215
You killed Bradford Wilcox
because he was going to accuse you

703
00:45:52,383 --> 00:45:56,377
of jury tampering in the
Andros Chemical Case.

704
00:45:56,654 --> 00:45:59,920
Mr. Howard, never
trick a trickster.

705
00:46:00,458 --> 00:46:01,619
We're both lawyers.

706
00:46:01,792 --> 00:46:07,129
We know that we do little shady
things to win our case. At least, I do.

707
00:46:07,298 --> 00:46:10,291
But jury tampering, a little
cross the line, you know.

708
00:46:10,468 --> 00:46:12,403
Wouldn't it have been better

709
00:46:12,937 --> 00:46:15,429
if Mr. Wilcox had charged
you with jury tampering?

710
00:46:15,606 --> 00:46:17,598
You'd have lost your
job and gone to prison,

711
00:46:17,775 --> 00:46:22,213
but now it looks like
you've killed your friend.

712
00:46:25,650 --> 00:46:27,312
It's a shame.

713
00:46:34,759 --> 00:46:36,557
Hey - Come on.

714
00:46:36,727 --> 00:46:37,956
Hi.

715
00:46:38,129 --> 00:46:40,223
I see Gavin $ Howard
are going out of business.

716
00:46:40,398 --> 00:46:42,458
Gone. Gone out of business.

717
00:46:42,633 --> 00:46:44,568
- Good to have you back.
- Good to be here.

718
00:46:44,735 --> 00:46:47,500
- You did a great job over there.
- Thank you.

719
00:46:47,672 --> 00:46:49,971
We need to do
something nice for him.

720
00:46:50,141 --> 00:46:53,134
Take him out to lunch or dinner.

721
00:46:53,311 --> 00:46:55,212
Dad, let's give him a raise.

722
00:46:56,814 --> 00:46:58,248
Raise?

723
00:46:58,416 --> 00:47:02,080
Well, just enough for
a corner room at the Y.

